{"status":"answered","answer":"Myopia, also known as nearsightedness, is a condition where a person can see nearby objects clearly but has difficulty seeing objects at a distance. This happens because the image of distant objects is formed in front of the retina instead of on it. To correct myopia, a concave lens is used. 

A concave lens diverges the light rays before they enter the eye, effectively moving the image back onto the retina. This allows the person to see distant objects clearly.

Unfortunately, the provided textbook evidence does not include a specific ray diagram for myopia correction. However, it mentions that using a concave lens can help bring the image of a distant object to a point between the far point and the point of least distance of distinct vision, allowing the eye lens to form a clear image on the retina.","selected_evidence_ids":["E3"],"citations":[{"evidence_id":"E3","pdf_page":101,"textbook_page":91}],"missing_information":["Specific ray diagram for myopia correction"]}
